---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Koji & Oncom - MycoLab">
  <div class="page-container">
    <h1 class="page-title">🌾 KOJI & ONCOM: SOLID-STATE FERMENTATION</h1>
    
    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>What is Solid-State Fermentation (SSF)?</h2>
      <p>Solid-State Fermentation utilizes microfungi (Ascomycetes and Zygomycetes) to transform low-value agricultural waste into nutrient-dense food products. This ancient technology addresses multiple challenges simultaneously:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Converts waste streams into high-protein foods</li>
        <li>Increases protein content significantly</li>
        <li>Enhances nutrient bioavailability</li>
        <li>Provides rapid production cycles (24-96 hours)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Koji (Aspergillus oryzae)</h2>
      
      <h3>Traditional Japanese Fermentation Starter</h3>
      <p>Koji is the cornerstone of Japanese fermentation culture, used to produce sake, miso, soy sauce, and amazake.</p>
      
      <h3>Substrates</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rice:</strong> Most common, produces delicate flavors</li>
        <li><strong>Barley:</strong> Traditional for miso production</li>
        <li><strong>Soybeans:</strong> Used for specific soy sauce types</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Enzyme Production</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Amylase:</strong> Breaks down starches into sugars</li>
        <li><strong>Protease:</strong> Breaks down proteins into amino acids</li>
        <li><strong>Result:</strong> Develops umami and enhances digestibility</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Modern Applications</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Rapid vegetable pickling</li>
        <li>Meat tenderization and aging</li>
        <li>Novel flavor development in plant proteins</li>
        <li>Waste valorization (agricultural byproducts)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Oncom (Neurospora spp.)</h2>
      
      <h3>Indonesian Fermented Food</h3>
      <p>Oncom is a vibrant orange fermented cake produced with Neurospora intermedia, traditionally grown on peanut press cake and okara (soybean pulp).</p>
      
      <h3>Substrates</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Peanut Press Cake:</strong> Traditional substrate</li>
        <li><strong>Okara (Tofu Waste):</strong> Soy pulp from tofu production</li>
        <li><strong>Cassava:</strong> Root vegetable waste</li>
        <li><strong>Other Agricultural Waste:</strong> Adaptable to local materials</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Characteristics</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Color:</strong> Bright orange from Neurospora pigments</li>
        <li><strong>Flavor:</strong> Nutty, earthy, umami-rich</li>
        <li><strong>Texture:</strong> Firm, sliceable cake</li>
        <li><strong>Production:</strong> 3-4 days fermentation cycle</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Nutritional Benefits</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Significant protein enhancement</li>
        <li>Vitamin synthesis (especially B vitamins)</li>
        <li>Improved digestibility of substrate</li>
        <li>High fiber content</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Tempeh (Rhizopus oligosporus)</h2>
      
      <h3>The Original Fermented Soybean Cake</h3>
      <p>While not covered in detail here, tempeh deserves mention as the most famous SSF food product, sharing production principles with oncom.</p>
      
      <h3>Key Features</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Production:</strong> 24-48 hours at 30-32°C</li>
        <li><strong>Protein Content:</strong> >40% potential (dry weight)</li>
        <li><strong>Quality Control:</strong> Standardized inoculum techniques</li>
        <li><strong>Global Adoption:</strong> Widely accepted plant-based protein</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Quality Control Challenge: Inoculum Standardization</h2>
      
      <h3>The Problem</h3>
      <p>Historically, oncom production occurs on a <strong>small-scale, home industry basis without specific inoculum</strong>, which results in unstable quality. This contrasts with tempeh, which typically employs standardized inoculum techniques leading to consistent results.</p>
      
      <h3>The Solution</h3>
      <p>Producing and tracking high-quality liquid cultures or spore preparations ensures:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Consistent product quality</li>
        <li>Predictable fermentation times</li>
        <li>Enhanced food safety</li>
        <li>Scalable production</li>
      </ul>
      
      <div class="highlight-box">
        <h4>💡 Mycolab Approach</h4>
        <p>By maintaining pure cultures of Neurospora and Rhizopus with rigorous tracking systems, we can standardize traditional SSF foods and ensure safety for community-scale production.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Production Comparison</h2>
      
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Product</th>
            <th>Fungus</th>
            <th>Typical Substrate</th>
            <th>Production Time</th>
            <th>Protein (Dry Wt)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Tempeh</td>
            <td>Rhizopus oligosporus</td>
            <td>Soybeans</td>
            <td>24-48 hours</td>
            <td>>40%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Oncom</td>
            <td>Neurospora spp.</td>
            <td>Peanut cake, okara</td>
            <td>3-4 days</td>
            <td>High (variable)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Koji</td>
            <td>Aspergillus oryzae</td>
            <td>Rice, barley, soy</td>
            <td>24-48 hours</td>
            <td>Enzyme production focus</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Modern Applications & Sustainable Food Production</h2>
      
      <h3>Waste Valorization</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Okara:</strong> 1 million tons produced annually in Indonesia alone</li>
        <li><strong>Peanut Press Cake:</strong> Protein-rich waste from oil extraction</li>
        <li><strong>Agricultural Residues:</strong> Cassava peels, corn steep liquor, etc.</li>
        <li><strong>Result:</strong> "Waste-to-wealth" circular economy</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Food Security Applications</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Rapid protein production in resource-limited settings</li>
        <li>Low capital requirements (no expensive equipment)</li>
        <li>Utilizes locally available materials</li>
        <li>Short production cycles enable quick response to needs</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Novel Food Development</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Alternative meat products using SSF</li>
        <li>Upcycled food ingredients</li>
        <li>Functional food additives</li>
        <li>Biofortification with vitamins/minerals</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Basic Production Protocol</h2>
      
      <h3>Step 1: Substrate Preparation</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Select and clean agricultural waste substrate</li>
        <li>Soak in water (4-12 hours depending on material)</li>
        <li>Steam or boil to pasteurize (60-90 minutes)</li>
        <li>Drain and cool to room temperature</li>
      </ol>
      
      <h3>Step 2: Inoculation</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Spread substrate in shallow trays (2-3cm deep)</li>
        <li>Sprinkle with starter culture (spores or liquid culture)</li>
        <li>Mix gently to distribute inoculum</li>
        <li>Cover with banana leaves, cloth, or perforated plastic</li>
      </ol>
      
      <h3>Step 3: Fermentation</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Maintain temperature 28-32°C</li>
        <li>Ensure adequate airflow (aerobic process)</li>
        <li>Monitor daily for mycelial growth</li>
        <li>Harvest when fully colonized (cake formation)</li>
      </ol>
      
      <h3>Step 4: Post-Processing</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Slice or crumble fermented product</li>
        <li>Can be consumed directly, fried, or steamed</li>
        <li>Sun-dry for extended storage if needed</li>
        <li>Package and label with production date</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Safety Considerations</h2>
      
      <div class="warning-box">
        <h4>⚠️ Critical Food Safety Points</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Use Pure Cultures:</strong> Wild contamination can include toxigenic fungi</li>
          <li><strong>Proper Pasteurization:</strong> Eliminates competing microorganisms</li>
          <li><strong>Clean Equipment:</strong> Prevents cross-contamination</li>
          <li><strong>Monitor pH:</strong> Correct pH prevents bacterial growth</li>
          <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Consume fresh or properly dry for shelf stability</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      
      <h3>MGAP Integration</h3>
      <p>All SSF production should follow Mushroom Good Agricultural Practices (MGAP) guidelines for worker hygiene, water safety, and product security.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Research & Development Opportunities</h2>
      
      <h3>Optimization Studies</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Substrate composition for maximum protein yield</li>
        <li>Temperature and humidity optimization</li>
        <li>Fermentation time vs. nutritional content</li>
        <li>Strain selection for specific substrates</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3>Novel Applications</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>SSF of coffee pulp waste</li>
        <li>Brewery spent grain fermentation</li>
        <li>Food waste upcycling programs</li>
        <li>Integrated farming systems</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="content-card">
      <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>✅ SSF transforms waste into high-protein food in 24-96 hours</li>
        <li>✅ Koji, oncom, and tempeh represent proven SSF technologies</li>
        <li>✅ Standardized inoculum essential for quality and safety</li>
        <li>✅ Minimal equipment required - accessible technology</li>
        <li>✅ Significant potential for food security in LMICs</li>
        <li>✅ Enzyme production (koji) enables unique flavor development</li>
        <li>✅ Circular economy: waste valorization creates economic value</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-container {
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem;
  }

  .page-title {
    font-family: 'Press Start 2P', monospace;
    font-size: 2rem;
    color: #9900ff;
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    text-shadow: 0 0 20px rgba(153, 0, 255, 0.5);
  }

  .content-card {
    background: #0a0a0a;
    border: 3px solid #9900ff;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    box-shadow: 0 0 20px rgba(153, 0, 255, 0.2);
  }

  .content-card h2 {
    font-family: 'Press Start 2P', monospace;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: #9900ff;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .content-card h3 {
    font-family: 'Press Start 2P', monospace;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: #cc66ff;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .content-card h4 {
    font-family: 'Press Start 2P', monospace;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: #ffaa00;
    margin: 1rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .content-card p {
    font-family: 'VT323', monospace;
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    color: #ffffff;
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .content-card ul, .content-card ol {
    font-family: 'VT323', monospace;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: #ffffff;
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-left: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .content-card li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .content-card strong {
    color: #cc66ff;
  }

  .warning-box {
    background: rgba(255, 170, 0, 0.1);
    border: 2px solid #ffaa00;
    padding: 1rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .highlight-box {
    background: rgba(153, 0, 255, 0.1);
    border: 2px solid #9900ff;
    padding: 1rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .data-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    font-family: 'VT323', monospace;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .data-table th {
    background: #9900ff;
    color: #000;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: bold;
  }

  .data-table td {
    border: 1px solid #9900ff;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .data-table tr:nth-child(even) {
    background: rgba(153, 0, 255, 0.05);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .page-container {
      padding: 1rem;
    }

    .page-title {
      font-size: 1.2rem;
    }

    .content-card {
      padding: 1.5rem;
    }

    .data-table {
      font-size: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
